From e6162f8f62d2bc46f9aff79fdf80e191460d1f53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: crazynucfan <127292955+crazynucfan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 21:50:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] bpf: Reject a non-scalar bpf_set_retval() argument with
 -EACCES

v7.2.8 gained a check that bpf_set_retval()'s argument is a scalar,
failing the program load with -EINVAL otherwise. Every other register
type mismatch in the verifier fails with -EACCES (check_reg_type()),
and before this check a pointer argument was accepted for privileged
programs.

The difference matters to feature probes. cilium/ebpf probes a helper
by loading a program that calls it with whatever the registers hold (R1
is the context pointer) and reads -EACCES as "the helper exists, the
arguments are wrong" and -EINVAL as "no such helper", or an unknown
failure. Cilium 1.20.1's agent probes bpf_set_retval for cgroup/sock
programs at startup and exits on this kernel:

  failed to probe helper ... FnSetRetval for program type CGroupSock:
  load program: invalid argument: ... R1 is not a scalar

Fail the check with -EACCES like the other type checks; programs that
pass a non-scalar are rejected as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 kernel/bpf/verifier.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/kernel/bpf/verifier.c b/kernel/bpf/verifier.c
index 1a0cd37b0..ab50121c3 100644
--- a/kernel/bpf/verifier.c
+++ b/kernel/bpf/verifier.c
@@ -10476,7 +10476,7 @@ static int check_helper_call(struct bpf_verifier_env *env, struct bpf_insn *insn
 
 		if (r1->type != SCALAR_VALUE) {
 			verbose(env, "R1 is not a scalar\n");
-			return -EINVAL;
+			return -EACCES;
 		}
 
 		/* CGROUP_GETSOCKOPT is allowed to return arbitrary value */
